  "worked_solution": "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{\u2032}+g({y}^{\u2032}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{\u2032}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{\u2032}+{y}^{\u2032}-2,\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{\u2032}+g({y}^{\u2032})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{\u2032})={y}^{\u2032}-2.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny={y}^{\u2032}-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x{y}^{\u2032}),\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{\u2032}+g({y}^{\u2032}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{\u2032}+{y}^{\u2032},\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{\u2032}+g({y}^{\u2032})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{\u2032})={y}^{\u2032}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
